 ```
 
-Password storage and token comparison live in `accounts.crypto`, which the reset
-flow calls rather than hashing or comparing anything itself. Issuing a token and
-checking one are implemented; completing a reset is not.
+## Reset flow
+
+1. **Request.** `request_reset(email)` issues a random 256-bit URL-safe token, records
+   when it was issued, and returns it for delivery to the account owner (e.g. by
+   email). Requesting again replaces any earlier outstanding token for that account.
+   The token is never logged.
+2. **Complete.** `complete_reset(email, token, new_password)` returns `True` and
+   stores the new password only if the token matches the one issued for that account
+   and is less than 30 minutes old. Otherwise it returns `False` and changes nothing;
+   it does not raise for unknown, used or expired tokens.
+3. **Single use.** A successful reset discards all outstanding reset tokens for the
+   account, so replaying the token returns `False`. An expired token is rejected
+   the same way.
+
+```python
+from accounts.reset import complete_reset, request_reset
+
+token = request_reset("user@example.com")
+complete_reset("user@example.com", token, "new passphrase")  # True
+complete_reset("user@example.com", token, "again")  # False, already used
+```
+
+`check_token(email, token)` validates a token (match and expiry) without consuming it.
+
+Password storage (scrypt with a per-user salt and a pepper) and constant-time token
+comparison live in `accounts.crypto`, which the reset flow calls rather than hashing or
+comparing anything itself.
